# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: autocad-com-fails-in-disconnected-windows-session — COM/GUI CAD dies when the runner's desktop is suspended, headless batch survives
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: ci-infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: cad-session-state-probe
#+DATE: 2026-09-28

* Observed

Two benchmark jobs on the SAME Windows runner (PF5S26B), the SAME minute
(2026-09-28 17:35–17:36Z):

| job                          | id          | result  | how far                              |
|------------------------------+-------------+---------+--------------------------------------|
| benchmark:bricscad:windows   | 16783323457 | success | all 7 benchmarks, summary, exit 0    |
| benchmark:autocad:windows    | 16783323458 | FAILED  | 2 of 7 (arithmetic, lists) then died |

AutoCAD was NOT a COM-startup failure: the trace shows =implementation :
Visual LISP 2022 (fr)=, i.e. AutoCAD launched and AutoLISP executed. It ran
=arithmetic= and =lists=, then the process died mid-suite (~5 s hang) and
=make -C autolisp-benchmark run-autocad-windows= returned Error 1. BricsCAD
(=LispEx : Version 25.0=) ran the whole suite to completion on the same
machine.

* Cause (pjb, from the machine)

"The runner user account is logged-in, but the GUI session is suspended, back
to the login window / change user." AutoCAD's benchmark path drives the
application by COM automation, and *COM automation of a GUI application needs
a live interactive desktop*. When the session is disconnected/locked, the
desktop is gone and the GUI-dependent parts of AutoCAD fault partway through.
BricsCAD's path completed before touching anything that needs the desktop, or
tolerated it.

In alfe terms this is the =--mode automation= (COM bridge via =cscript=) vs
=--mode batch= (=accoreconsole=, headless, no COM) divide documented in the
alfe user manual: automation needs the desktop, batch does not. =--epure=
drives COM on AutoCAD, so it is in the automation camp.

* What this ticket adds

Not a fix for AutoCAD -- the runner's session state is an environment fact,
not a code defect -- but the instrumentation to SEE it and to characterise
what survives it:

1. =scripts/windows-session-state.ps1= — prints the interactive-session
   state: process vs console session id, WTS connect state (Active /
   Disconnected / …), window-station name (=console= vs =rdp-tcp#N=), and
   whether =LogonUI.exe= is up (locked / at the login screen). It ends with a
   one-line, greppable =SESSION-STATE: transport=… connect=… locked=… =>
   VERDICT= and always exits 0.

2. Every Windows CAD job now logs it. =.cad-sweep-before= (which every
   Windows CAD job extends) runs it FIRST, before the orphan sweep, so a CAD
   job's log says whether the desktop was live -- turning "Error 1" into
   "Error 1, and the desktop was DISCONNECTED".

3. =probe:cad-session-state:windows= (manual, CAD-tagged) — runs a 3x3
   matrix in the current session state: {clautolisp (in-process control),
   bricscad, autocad} x {=--mode batch=, =--mode automation=, =--epure=},
   recording each cell's exit + marker + whether it reached READY, and prints
   a table to read against the session banner. It reports and exits 0.

* Expected shape of the result (hypothesis, to confirm by running the probe)

- clautolisp: OK in every state (in-process, no desktop).
- =--mode batch=: OK even disconnected (accoreconsole is headless; BricsCAD
  batch already proven so by benchmark:bricscad:windows).
- =--mode automation= / =--epure=: FAIL or REACHED-READY-THEN-FAILED while
  disconnected/locked; OK on a live desktop.

If confirmed, the operational conclusion is that COM/GUI CAD jobs need the
runner's desktop kept CONNECTED (e.g. an RDP session left attached, or
autologon without lock), and that automated CAD verification that must run
unattended should prefer the batch/accoreconsole path where the vendor offers
one.

* RDP note

The detector distinguishes transport (=console= vs =rdp-tcp#N=) and connect
state, because RDP has its own version of this: an =rdp-tcp= session is
=Active= while the client is attached and =Disconnected= the moment the client
detaches WITHOUT logging off -- the same desktop-gone condition as a locked
console, and it breaks COM CAD the same way. A CAD job that works while
someone is RDP'd in and fails after they disconnect is this, not flakiness.

* Acceptance criteria

- =probe:cad-session-state:windows= run in the disconnected state produces a
  matrix distinguishing the engines/modes that survive it from those that do
  not, read against a =SESSION-STATE:= line that correctly names the state.
- Every Windows CAD job's log contains the =SESSION-STATE:= line.
- The detector correctly reports =locked= at the login screen and the
  =rdp-tcp= transport under RDP (confirmed on the machine).

* Note

Filed while comparing benchmark:autocad:windows with
benchmark:bricscad:windows at pjb's request. The scripts run only on the
Windows runner; they are validated there, not in the Linux lanes.

* Merged in: alfe-autocad-createobject-fails-on-runner (closed 2026-09-28)

The same morning, every =alfe --autocad= COM run in =verify:epure-api:windows=
(MR !336, job 16774481586) waited ~120 s and exited 4 with =ATTACHED=0
CREATED=0= -- =CreateObject= failed -- while BricsCAD ran on the same machine;
in the afternoon (MR !341, job 16776999021, alfe 2.2.146) AutoCAD started in
every run. A suspended desktop explains both. If it recurs, alfe >= 2.2.146
prints the COM error (it was being dropped: CP1252 error file read as UTF-8,
alfe-autocad-bridge-com-error-dropped-on-ansi); ~120 s then 0x80080005
(CO_E_SERVER_EXEC_FAILURE) would confirm it.

One thing to check here, noted when the two were compared:
=benchmark:autocad:windows= runs =--mode batch= (accoreconsole, headless, no
COM -- =autolisp-benchmark/Makefile=, =run-autocad-windows=), so its
mid-suite death may need another explanation than the missing desktop.
